th} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396364} {:answer/id #uuid "36245680-7638-50b4-92e7-b19bd38f18ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396365} {:answer/id #uuid "7aec96f3-5ac2-5f12-9c3b-19206e678262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396366} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec5c43f-8d6e-5310-8342-46da810d390a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5d52a01-8196-5434-9481-2eaebc93f7cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396363} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cc9d7c1-5452-5aef-b74f-2d05199272c5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6acd5ad6-cecd-50bc-9edc-fc2a85e9b7ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-334652" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =5(0.5)^{4}-(0.5)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.062\\,5-1\\cdot 0.031\\,25 \\\\  & =0.281\\,25 \\\\  & ≈0.281.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.25) & =5(0.25)^{4}-(0.25)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.003\\,90...-1\\cdot 0.000\\,97... \\\\  & =0.018\\,55... \\\\  & ≈0.019.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,25$ to $0.018\\,55....$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.281\\,25}{0.018\\,55...}=15.157\\,89…≈15.158.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈15.157\\,89… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(15.157\\,89…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.922… \\\\ p & ≈2.922….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})=O\\,((Δx)^{3+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe4292f-7653-5486-975e-c206acae734e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of Big-O"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9b0a713-2b61-57c1-9f9d-58f3cfe127bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396369} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc26a4b-7299-5453-a613-a9ae910256f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396370} {:answer/id #uuid "4a66523f-3f6e-5362-8693-1be5e891a202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9b0a713-2b61-57c1-9f9d-58f3cfe127bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396369} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebab8af0-912f-518c-a7e1-56fb2f6c22c6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396368} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a3b801f-1e63-569f-ae46-91f284776563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396373} {:answer/id #uuid "127ae45d-ffab-54e2-9daa-8211b2dba34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396374} {:answer/id #uuid "0a800b7b-873a-54c5-b9e4-348dc51786b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396375} {:answer/id #uuid "835341e5-4838-59d1-9827-cecc9da89d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396376} {:answer/id #uuid "2e746387-c030-55e6-b555-52d025a3cd29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a3b801f-1e63-569f-ae46-91f284776563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396373} :answer-field/id #uuid "db90e99a-7cc6-5e69-be9d-461629831381" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396372} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a420298b-b257-5b95-ab12-3166223474cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396379} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba268ed-b53f-5f86-afdf-a086184c580d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396380} {:answer/id #uuid "c30092b5-a754-5b38-9153-f687a364a112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396381} {:answer/id #uuid "86f8bd2b-5b1c-5171-937e-306c91b5a43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396382} {:answer/id #uuid "0bebf996-bf50-547c-9801-32254a7b4157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ba268ed-b53f-5f86-afdf-a086184c580d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396380} :answer-field/id #uuid "869f87d8-d462-5ef0-919e-8c5fd622fb0b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396378} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab5d3c4f-33de-5eef-8ae7-7817054b32b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396385} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb05416-e870-500e-9a56-00e679e530ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbb05416-e870-500e-9a56-00e679e530ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396386} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd4e31a3-2c33-5488-8129-ccd3b7edb5c9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396384} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "168810da-b39a-596e-97d5-9d3f2cee753e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396388} {:answer/id #uuid "5191241f-ad91-527c-9c7d-fd7612a78fdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396389} {:answer/id #uuid "8e35eb45-ae11-5d49-8ed6-d440fcdf4c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396390} {:answer/id #uuid "047e1746-629c-5d4a-bc0b-01f3e0f827c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396391} {:answer/id #uuid "3beef058-c56a-5a74-a72d-b0d8e2526d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3beef058-c56a-5a74-a72d-b0d8e2526d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396392} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2436d9b-713f-53b8-9755-f99ff60b4717" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "189a55f5-72ee-590d-b213-1f6bb96fbc77" :question/math-academy-id "q-334685" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=3{e}^{2x}+2x,$ then $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below\n\n $\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2x}{3{e}^{2x}})=0$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,2x=O(3{e}^{2x})$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x})$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O({e}^{2x})$ \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{3{e}^{2x}+2x}_{⏟}}_{f})\n$$\n\nand this is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3},$ we have\n\n$$\n2x=O(3{e}^{2x}).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and the $\\text{absorption property},$ we have \n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)=O(3{e}^{2x}).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x})\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O({e}^{2x}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a98936b-f6f9-53c3-aefc-f49cb4b5c873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.865" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396395} {:answer/id #uuid "55c0