/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396400} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5867c6d-a295-5ff3-b71a-09896abbfaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396407} {:answer/id #uuid "6166969c-b258-5bd3-ba33-4d6c2b5260e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396408} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb003c5-48a6-5b4e-94b0-2117dee195f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396409} {:answer/id #uuid "467258ad-829b-56de-9a60-aa8344ebec1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396410} {:answer/id #uuid "c00bad96-4407-51c5-acb8-fcb6b6e99a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5867c6d-a295-5ff3-b71a-09896abbfaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396407} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c2a3a20-1dd1-5ddb-a48d-682bcbe0ae05" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396406} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c85e00a-95ff-524f-8ac8-afa17f026145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396413} {:answer/id #uuid "3afe21f4-ed72-5034-a318-429d736720ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396414} {:answer/id #uuid "273c3c59-532c-5cc4-b6be-48dd7002f743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396415} {:answer/id #uuid "db9905da-4464-50dd-bd5d-35d85ba054dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396416} {:answer/id #uuid "af475e5d-43c3-5c3c-9a30-02e92d61c3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3afe21f4-ed72-5034-a318-429d736720ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396414} :answer-field/id #uuid "027eda39-8180-548c-9212-c37a8b75ea4d" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4af4f8d-8886-57f9-92e8-20fda99f6128" :question/math-academy-id "q-334709" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.3$ to $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.3) & =4(0.3)^{3}-(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.027-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.108-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.099\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.1) & =4(0.1)^{3}-(0.1)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.001-0.000\\,1 \\\\  & =0.004-0.000\\,1 \\\\  & =0.003\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.099\\,9$ to $0.003\\,9.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.099\\,9}{0.003\\,9}=25.615\\,38…≈25.615.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈25.615\\,38… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(25.615\\,38…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈2.952… \\\\ p & ≈1.952….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$2.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})=O\\,((Δx)^{2+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17cc96c1-7c53-5909-a9eb-4804b7e498d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.057" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396420} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb02a74-9fae-5992-999d-2d580bdb32f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.420" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396421} {:answer/id #uuid "f389774e-2155-5122-83ea-27a9212a7e57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396422} {:answer/id #uuid "cad7653e-3452-5b8d-9ce7-c46d36f9a267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396423} {:answer/id #uuid "78583d61-1a17-5f4f-be43-b20d831cfe8a" :answer/type {:db/iden