er.type/math} :answer/value "0.420" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396434} {:answer/id #uuid "ac032cf4-1eab-5c84-8f18-d0a5924193bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396435} {:answer/id #uuid "e363d853-d1c5-51d6-a0fb-2443f46b88cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "240415c7-e878-52d3-9818-987c16a52c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.057" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396432} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bcdfbc2-20ee-5a87-bcbb-bbc15ceea9ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03939d3d-10e1-5542-975f-44bddcd2dbd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396438} {:answer/id #uuid "df86ffe6-433e-5430-bebd-53eb76e7e9bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396439} {:answer/id #uuid "0775949f-6d2f-54c2-9c2a-de12bbc14457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396440} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c61c25-2ca8-58fd-9ac9-1449675bc5cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396441} {:answer/id #uuid "2108043e-2d89-52c0-8e45-c21a315bde10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2c61c25-2ca8-58fd-9ac9-1449675bc5cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396441} :answer-field/id #uuid "88065a7c-eae9-5a5f-80b4-2a12c5d1208c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "191ac454-645f-542b-be97-450b5f3d1a65" :question/math-academy-id "q-334710" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =6(0.6)^{5}-(0.6)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.077\\,76-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.466\\,56-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.419\\,904 \\\\  & ≈0.420.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.4) & =6(0.4)^{5}-(0.4)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.010\\,24-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.061\\,44-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.057\\,344 \\\\  & ≈0.057.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.4.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.4}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={1.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.419\\,904$ to $0.057\\,344.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.419\\,904}{0.057\\,344}=7.322\\,54…≈7.323.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1.5}^{p+1} & ≈7.322\\,54… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{1.5}⁡(7.322\\,54…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.910… \\\\ p & ≈3.910….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b510a71-8c9b-527d-9bcb-f5fbb3d49293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396445} {:answer/id #uuid "fb33a210-7096-5874-a3f2-db5201cb5c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396446} {:answer/id #uuid "7357b661-3a8e-586f-b745-87bada056b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.281" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396447} {:answer/id #uuid "368eff5d-c023-5e5b-be1f-e7e458e23832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396448} {:answer/id #uuid "07ef22c7-7e79-5318-91fd-2a287e7cf0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb33a210-7096-5874-a3f2-db5201cb5c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396446} :answer-field/id #uuid "d920cb15-c78d-5c51-9a36-297e1002feed" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396444} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af30b4d5-ae13-5e76-aef4-2c8e10d268d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.016" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396451} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4d56fd-96cd-5769-9c15-8ba42c5adc88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.281" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396452} {:answer/id #uuid "14a16a56-934d-5ec6-8e25-d2388421e668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396453} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6cdaa2-9b9d-5112-9911-3369aa53afb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396454} {:answer/id #uuid "a158f595-cfa4-5af1-86e9-3373c27d36fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d4d56fd-96cd-5769-9c15-8ba42c5adc88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.281" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396452} :answer-field/id #uuid "22993398-663b-5a03-abf5-707daa93f877" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396450} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6e06075-4e2d-569c-b93f-165663c39144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99.068" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396457} {:answer/id #uuid "f089d8dd-6f47-565b-ab6c-d6ae32100108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.385" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396458} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8aca3b-0007-5700-a38f-b489a38acf4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396459} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2c50b5-edcd-59fc-87f9-4fe83145da6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "107.403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396460} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fcccd7-bafd-5c90-975c-73cad92fbe95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104.632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3fcccd7-bafd-5c90-975c-73cad92fbe95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104.632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396461} :answer-field/id #uuid "b053a512-93f4-5783-be56-305d82bb8d4b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85b7c966-24c0-5f9e-ba19-26d61189aa8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396463} {:answer/id #uuid "b09802b2-85c8-57e5-8e51-14a3469d9ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396464} {:answer/id #uuid "7a131701-833d-535b-800c-3887b7fc4a46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396465} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d8158d-f371-5bb8-9048-14d8a5c82403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396466} {:answer/id #uuid "48d8e74d-7332-5d6c-91a3-aaeed8c4c07d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2d8158d-f371-5bb8-9048-14d8a5c82403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396466} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a5e6e5a-102a-53f8-be70-6fec86c3cf6a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c78fbb9-dc1c-5d53-be56-9ae880822440" :question/math-academy-id "q-334724" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =8(0.5)^{5}+2(0.5)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.031\\,25+2\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =0.281\\,25 \\\\  & ≈0.281.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.2) & =8(0.2)^{5}+2(0.2)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.000\\,32+2\\cdot 0.000\\,064 \\\\  & =0.002\\,688 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.2}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,25$ to $0.002\\,688.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.281\\,25}{0.002\\,688}=104.631\\,69…≈104.632.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2.5}^{p+1} & ≈104.631\\,69… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(104.631\\,69…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈5.075… \\\\ p & ≈4.075….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9c2220-030e-5418-915c-e34d7962c077" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing a Solution of the Lotka-Volterra Model Given the Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6bc298-819c-5e72-8a93-83f08ec194c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396469} {:answer/id #uuid "8c621ff2-0784-5f4a-9f17-554f017a4290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c621ff2-0784-5f4a-9f17-554f017a4290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396470} :answer-field/id #uuid "a630ba98-1096-5e16-a8ba-c72404307d8b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396468} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6ca0294-aea3-59be-887c-efcec2803859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "circles around" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396472} {:answer/id #uuid "b05325f4-1482-56b6-8efe-ae71cef71608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "approaches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6ca0294-aea3-59be-887c-efcec2803859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "circles around" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396472} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a3c7d81-1bee-5ec7-956c-8dc539d9a494" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6db0a17e-67bc-5337-9db7-aa8831e48825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396475} {:answer/id #uuid "285c3558-f151-51cd-8f5f-b1b41cc764e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db0a17e-67bc-5337-9db7-aa8831e48825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396475} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6eebb0b-3bd1-5fdc-aab2-c16f8bfe569f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396474} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ff91e14-b9a5-586b-b8da-fd89429ce569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the nonzero equilibrium" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396478} {:answer/id #uuid "d16001f2-8334-5a4d-a5d3-2705ca6416c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the point of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396479} {:answer/id #uuid "a5776bca-8e30-5c52-81a1-7c0faeb65e18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff91e14-b9a5-586b-b8da-fd89429ce569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the nonzero equilibrium" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396478} :answer-field/id #uuid "af8b3b57-c322-5cc0-b2e2-876f370e51d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc687ab5-118b-5439-986b-20786139182f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334754" :question/problem "![](images/71/718df02f2d0c2f01cc4a02a40da120178afe0f436bbf38cd5a8d249d87b5d1c9.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t),y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n$\\,$\nThe trajectory \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements:\n\n- The trajectory $\\text{circles around}$ $\\text{the nonzero equilibrium}.$\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey $\\text{decreases}$ and the number of predators $\\text{increases}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cf9f192-aef0-5fea-9c8d-2c9d0d2437d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396483} {:answer/id #uuid "f5085233-b908-5501-a0c5-0dcd0ec2f11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396484} {:answer/id #uuid "24a0f0c5-225e-579b-ac73-6b945a917dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396485} {:answer/id #uuid "524fe3fc-f53f-5d1f-94a1-241f9721d007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "524fe3fc-f53f-5d1f-94a1-241f9721d007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396486} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbef4659-4a1b-5336-bc24-105bca59f1da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396482} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86b4b496-6913-51e5-9295-17ccd130a975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign again" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396488} {:answer/id #uuid "907b8f18-b9ed-5872-840b-9d9473ea9eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396489} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb530cd-55c8-5894-93a8-3bf2dcbf1c6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cb530cd-55c8-5894-93a8-3bf2dcbf1c6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396490} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa3586e-c7c4-513b-8443-0289c8a062dc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396487} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bcdc459-83a5-50fe-be39-07376cdff9a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the chain rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396492} {:answer/id #uuid "3106cae2-aa39-50e5-a759-99c5161fc219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396493} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4df89d-8fd7-5702-a951-30eefa0bde62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the product rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3106cae2-aa39-50e5-a759-99c5161fc219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396493} :answer-field/id #uuid "1936914d-83ba-5f97-8535-9faf43b5be4e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70e96342-9f6f-5f4d-885c-91889dc5a387" :question/math-academy-id "q-334829" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-L{t\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ and\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}F(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nLine $\\text{L4}$ tells us that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the integrand in line $\\text{L3}$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-t{e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substitution}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L6}.$ By $\\text{the definition of the Laplace transform},$\n\n$$\nL{t\\cdot f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}(tf(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the integral in line $\\text{L5}$, we see that\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}(tf(t))\\,dt=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)},\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the expression in line $\\text{L6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc76cf1-03a1-556f-8931-c7d7ae719952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the linearity of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396496} {:answer/id #uuid "88f09b51-6c19-5b71-8fd7-eed9401b1f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the chain rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396497} {:answer/id #uuid "97a57549-7948-51f0-8af3-6bc0134199b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the product rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88f09b51-6c19-5b71-8fd7-eed9401b1f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the chain rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396497} :answer-field/id #uuid "a33fd5fc-c3b7-5ab6-a7f4-eb3d87c63a29" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396495} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f30aa404-e4f2-5373-9440-0f582fdf6308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the continuity of partial derivatives" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396500} {:answer/id #uuid "256516bd-7cb5-5b7b-bbf7-7ea382627c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396501} {:answer/id #uuid "43a8fd0c-acff-57a1-9314-99515a158b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "256516bd-7cb5-5b7b-bbf7-7ea382627c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396501} :answer-field/id #uuid "650089ca-dc92-58ba-ade7-b9216277e146" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396499} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049161f2-2814-5e0d-ad2c-e802e9b9b233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396504} {:answer/id #uuid "851c36f7-f486-5c90-9b67-e065924120c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396505} {:answer/id #uuid "edca89f0-6897-5921-883e-d990e3fa90e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396506} {:answer/id #uuid "b02e7b04-a39b-5681-8e01-11a62924acca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edca89f0-6897-5921-883e-d990e3fa90e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396506} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ada3d2-1029-5346-a7b6-61db0391ecdc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40ff4752-2fb6-5924-9814-2ff991abb48a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334874" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the second derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}F(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L4}.$ The function $f(t)$ does not depend on $s$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))=f(t)\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,\\frac{∂}{∂s}(-t{e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,({t}^{2}{e}^{-st})={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nThus line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{the chain rule}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb0ad98-5f4e-54e0-9915-621ebc30cb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396510} {:answer/id #uuid "0cbb1d3c-3189-59cd-9bda-454fb8b2eebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396511} {:answer/id #uuid "451fca8a-ec8c-5281-94c2-dddf8802efbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396512} {:answer/id #uuid "050780e1-44f8-5c5a-b0da-175e8229eda4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "451fca8a-ec8c-5281-94c2-dddf8802efbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396512} :answer-field/id #uuid "c67de78c-6154-54d9-8558-c3b040029d23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396509} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eda48670-807f-5f46-a959-ae799864f963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the continuity of partial derivatives" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396515} {:answer/id #uuid "36aed3da-5225-5da6-936b-33aff7769869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396516} {:answer/id #uuid "1c885eda-c55a-5a9d-944c-a3ff564b42df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating by parts with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36aed3da-5225-5da6-936b-33aff7769869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396516} :answer-field/id #uuid "3341eb97-3091-56ff-8e4d-1ad36960514a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396514} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c131cc8-9ab8-5462-9e4c-380e2f0a5574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign again" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396519} {:answer/id #uuid "0907155f-a18b-5db1-b522-3b3edc7da6d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396520} {:answer/id #uuid "85dade51-274f-5498-850c-ab5af3ef7514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85dade51-274f-5498-850c-ab5af3ef7514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396521} :answer-field/id #uuid "75b69391-8c29-5787-b8c3-1abe4a208951" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b669529-7292-59be-8712-06b8b95e08ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-334879" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then $F(s)$ is twice differentiable for all $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$  \n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the second derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}F(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nLine $\\text{L4}$ tells us that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the integrand in line $\\text{L3}$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substitution}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "724c6608-ba55-593a-b831-9342a1ad2fe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396524} {:answer/id #uuid "6d012455-60c7-55cd-8abe-a5ae87c14843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396525} {:answer/id #uuid "f37ded06-8f51-502f-9066-f2e3021a9d5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396526} {:answer/id #uuid "61927b3b-fbb4-54e5-bcfd-ea2b2a79e95e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d012455-60c7-55cd-8abe-a5ae87c14843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396525} :answer-field/id #uuid "03a43476-4b63-5b9c-a60e-49518b095eb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396523} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110c49bf-db59-5807-8c76-e8a356cd26d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1))-3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396529} {:answer/id #uuid "f04a3ae3-f98a-5042-9303-276b057692c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1))+3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396530} {:answer/id #uuid "8c8f3e39-d3c1-5e7f-a209-959cacdc0344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396531} {:answer/id #uuid "b60dd011-c5bd-5d35-bf92-6b2b7e46ffc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1))+3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c8f3e39-d3c1-5e7f-a209-959cacdc0344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396531} :answer-field/id #uuid "309e3e22-428f-58e4-8c8d-2ab6315d5bb2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1bc76f0-5438-50ce-a2c1-3d7bf5ef80b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334924" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1))$ |  | $\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | ${e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s))$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1)) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1)) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cc0f169-e729-5947-84b4-6f07c528a50a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396535} {:answer/id #uuid "cb165ac4-0779-5002-85a2-b9755f87c486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cc0f169-e729-5947-84b4-6f07c528a50a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396535} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2ba4f30-23bd-5905-838d-76fd7661d248" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396534} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa00c12d-1fa3-53a3-bd19-48333bc426a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396538} {:answer/id #uuid "f44c0c3a-0dbe-50fc-b4db-26d4b4679d7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396539} {:answer/id #uuid "bed3fb95-e63c-5cb4-9873-36c7021753ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396540} {:answer/id #uuid "16ccee9d-9901-5cdd-8f6c-86a2537ed97a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa00c12d-1fa3-53a3-bd19-48333bc426a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396538} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e0f53c6-25f9-51a9-939e-8935f3db8e60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dcedf1a-f3f7-5345-8ad7-4690098e1077" :question/math-academy-id "q-334942" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.The Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(1,1)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y \\\\ 2x & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(1,1) & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1))\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16481b80-9744-5c8d-843a-14af30fd8cf4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying and Interpreting the Lotka-Volterra Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75e05438-3ae9-58ec-903e-82743e42d013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "rate of change" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396544} {:answer/id #uuid "707ed04f-5cf8-578a-8d26-c6b4f7655729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "amount" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75e05438-3ae9-58ec-903e-82743e42d013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "rate of change" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396544} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6a8c10-9c97-5d0e-a7ca-88d3123f21e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396543} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c53b9aee-4b74-5b15-9c02-23b4c28311c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "predator" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396547} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ecbe30-532a-5642-ac26-0feb1ebd151e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "prey" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c53b9aee-4b74-5b15-9c02-23b4c28311c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "predator" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396547} :answer-field/id #uuid "e00831ee-a120-529f-9a49-7553956b9af8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1189d035-9d29-5b3d-9e34-9bcf6834053f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334988" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-1.5x+2.5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=5y-1.2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.$\\,$\nTerm ${x}^{′}$ in the first equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${x}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=-1.5x+2.5xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the $\\text{predator}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it decays exponentially in the absence of prey (the $-1.5x$ term), and\n\n- increases when interacting with prey (the $2.5xy$ term)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9c2220-030e-5418-915c-e34d7962c077" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing a Solution of the Lotka-Volterra Model Given the Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f2218ff-6591-53f7-bbb3-3a4fefeb7720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "circles around" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396550} {:answer/id #uuid "db34f585-7424-54b4-824a-385921f24a40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "approaches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f2218ff-6591-53f7-bbb3-3a4fefeb7720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "circles around" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396550} :answer-field/id #uuid "a014f4d5-5a11-52db-b0bc-b00c033de4bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396549} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67c360e5-d05b-5518-ab25-0451a566e984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396553} {:answer/id #uuid "7172f1dc-100b-5720-bf88-72e70ee271bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67c360e5-d05b-5518-ab25-0451a566e984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396553} :answer-field/id #uuid "06d8940f-e44c-5f6d-b85c-7081bb725fa7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396552} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb1f08a9-6205-51cc-8c7b-0a999dbbe124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the nonzero equilibrium" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396556} {:answer/id #uuid "2d38dbb3-2e70-52af-b3c3-6bacbd921f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the point of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396557} {:answer/id #uuid "15de982d-6624-5d1d-ae5e-7e806bbbf18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb1f08a9-6205-51cc-8c7b-0a999dbbe124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the nonzero equilibrium" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396556} :answer-field/id #uuid "a001b1d9-a5cc-5a52-b1dc-c6f26819b75b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396555} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7765c9d7-0e20-5300-8678-86ebb862eb5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396560} {:answer/id #uuid "5d1825a3-5ffb-52b1-8b17-c6fa1547fd34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7765c9d7-0e20-5300-8678-86ebb862eb5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396560} :answer-field/id #uuid "80014b96-58a1-540f-b4b9-40b8251a49a0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4850b2b-4a19-5e29-aed4-55be99e7b3ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-335040" :question/problem "![](images/35/359b56411d4e215ef0ed423f6280a9e3641d5806d525b78e97569aaa02afe72f.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=8x-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t),y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n$\\,$\nThe trajectory \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements:\n\n- The trajectory $\\text{circles around}$ $\\text{the nonzero equilibrium}.$\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey $\\text{decreases}$ and the number of predators $\\text{decreases}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0095bbe3-9652-5054-b2e6-b54c94a109af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396564} {:answer/id #uuid "d480a481-e03a-5469-92c4-5278b1f7fdc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ \\pi  & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396565} {:answer/id #uuid "36e273ce-dbcd-5625-b775-6f6dcaa90c70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396566} {:answer/id #uuid "e50c9797-8311-56db-9e8e-13eb031dd8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d480a481-e03a-5469-92c4-5278b1f7fdc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ \\pi  & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396565} :answer-field/id #uuid "15699797-263d-5146-820d-3a7ce8d5cda0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396563} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b39ab91-1fd9-505d-8a6f-a0eae97c2dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396569} {:answer/id #uuid "66231965-9438-53a7-8707-d460c9b3ab3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66231965-9438-53a7-8707-d460c9b3ab3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396570} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f0af58-6ec9-52a4-a9b1-c5ea22e61b52" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2c782c5-e338-5c8a-884e-7da2e7513b5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335099" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.The Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,1)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) & -\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\pi y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,1) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) & -\\pi (0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) \\\\ \\pi (1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (0)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (0))\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ \\pi  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0-0\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eca32dd-1563-55c6-b594-08588efd601b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "445c6a63-f339-5ff2-afa4-e26785fbf1f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396573} {:answer/id #uuid "96de3917-2210-5111-9750-f9437e72cfa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396574} {:answer/id #uuid "875915a9-2dd7-5bfd-a876-9d427aa8a55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396575} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2a0cc9-e777-59ad-9b3f-6332bbbb90e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96de3917-2210-5111-9750-f9437e72cfa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396574} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e1c7d9a-e955-5ffd-bbe8-c5635e09bdc8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2b8ad61-9b47-5971-9495-e31b2ea4691b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335175" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2,\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
